Python has several built-in data types, including:
Numeric Types: int, float, complex
Boolean Type: bool
Sequence Types: list, tuple, range
Text Type: str
Set Types: set, frozenset
Mapping Type: dict
Binary Types: bytes, bytearray, memoryview
Each of these data types has its own specific properties and uses in Python.

Iterative datatypes in Python are data types that can be iterated over, meaning their elements can be accessed one by one. Examples of iterative data types in Python include:
Lists - A collection of ordered and changeable elements. It is represented using square brackets [].
Tuples - A collection of ordered and unchangeable elements. It is represented using parentheses ().
Strings - A collection of characters. It is represented using quotes ('' or "").
Sets - A collection of unordered and unindexed elements. It is represented using curly braces {}.
Dictionaries - A collection of unordered, changeable, and indexed elements in key-value pairs. It is represented using curly braces {} with keys and values separated by a colon (:).

In Python, a module is a file containing Python definitions and statements that can be imported into other Python programs.
To import a module in Python, we use the import statement, followed by the module name. For example, to import the math module, we write:
import math

We can also import specific functions from a module using the from keyword. For example, to import the sqrt() function from the math module, we write:
from math import sqrt

To rename a module in Python, we use the as keyword. For example, to import the math module and rename it to m, we write:
import math as m

To get the location of a module, we can use the __file__ attribute. For example, to get the location of the math module, we write:
import math
print(math.__file__)

The Python Standard Library provides a set of built-in modules that can be imported and used in our programs. Some of the commonly used built-in modules in Python include:
os: Provides a way of using operating system dependent functionality like reading or writing to the file system.
sys: Provides access to some variables used or maintained by the interpreter and to functions that interact strongly with the interpreter.
datetime: Provides classes for working with dates and times.
random: Provides functions for generating random numbers and sequences.
re: Provides regular expression matching operations.
and many more.

In Linux, you can check the IP address of your system using the ip command. Here's how to use it:
Open the terminal.
Type the following command and press Enter:
ip address
This command will display detailed information about the network interfaces on your system, including their IP addresses.
Alternatively, you can also use the ifconfig command to check the IP address. However, the ifconfig command is being deprecated in favor of ip and may not be available on all Linux distributions. To use ifconfig, simply type ifconfig in the terminal and press Enter.


Here are some commonly used network commands in Linux:
ip: Displays and manipulates the network interfaces, routing tables, and more.
ifconfig: Configures and displays the network interfaces on the system.
ping: Tests the connectivity between two hosts on a network by sending packets and measuring the response time.
traceroute: Traces the network path between two hosts and displays the hops along the way.
netstat: Displays active network connections, listening ports, and routing tables.
ss: Another command to display active network connections and sockets.
route: Displays and modifies the system's routing table.
nslookup: Queries a DNS server to resolve domain names to IP addresses.
dig: Another tool for DNS queries, with more advanced options than nslookup.
host: A simple tool for DNS queries that displays the IP address of a hostname.
nmap: A powerful network scanner and port scanner that can discover hosts and services on a network.
These are just a few examples of the many network commands available in Linux. Depending on your specific needs, there may be other commands or tools that are more appropriate for your use case.
